Run the full example:

```bash
signalflow examples/simple-circuit/back-and-forth.yaml
signalflow examples/simple-circuit/back-and-forth.yaml --zone 1,2 --wiring
signalflow examples/simple-circuit/back-and-forth.yaml --zone 1,2 --geometry
```

## Innovations

### World Geometry Zones

SignalFlow treats whitespace as owned space. A board is divided into terminal, fan, longitude, latitude, medial, extra, and transition regions with named roles such as `Wt`, `Wfi`, `Wi`, `Ni`, `Ei`, `Efi`, and `Et`. Placement and routing read from these regions, so layout is not a late rendering trick. If a route needs terminal approach clearance or fan-out depth, the zone must reserve it before materialization.

### Symbolic Circuit Notation

The visual language is compact but semantic. `►` and `◄` are direction markers. `╫` and `╪` are module-boundary pierce glyphs. `╔═ ║ ═╝` boxes are module boundaries. Signal labels attach to ports but are not route glyphs. Chip-local internal wiring uses declarations such as `c1ret:c2sig` to show how an incoming return becomes a later outgoing signal.

### Deterministic Wiring Algebra

Every rendered cell is the result of an algebraic composition rule. Horizontal and vertical wires form deterministic crossings; wires crossing module walls produce pierce glyphs; route direction markers stay present; same-axis overshoot backtracks are collapsed before rendering. This makes the output testable as structure rather than judged as art.

### Symbolic Routing Solutions

The board solver uses named route families instead of freehand drawing. A west-to-east intra-zone signal can be reasoned about as a symbolic path through `Wfi -> Wi -> Ni -> Ei -> Efi`; outer arcs use extra shells such as `We`, `Ne`, `Ee`, and `Se`. These solutions can be inspected before they are projected into ASCII, which is how routing bugs are localized to placement, solve, realization, or composition.

### Canonical Chip Graph

Nested YAML is only the source notation. The engine normalizes it into canonical chips and edges. A full `module` + `func` block declares a chip; a later short block references that chip; recursion and ancestor calls become edges back to existing chips. This is how one function can be drawn once while still receiving multiple routed calls.

### Terminal Landing Geometry

Labels are annotations, while direction markers and wire glyphs are structure. Long terminal labels reserve real approach space before fan and longitude routing is placed. That rule prevents labels from masquerading as routing corridors and prevents return terminals from looking orphaned when dense landings collide near a chip edge.

### Inspectable World Physics

The CLI and debugger can print geometry, wiring, selected zones, harmonized zone offsets, and snippet contexts. Final ASCII is late evidence; geometry views show what the engine believed before drawing. This matters because many layout bugs look like bad glyphs but actually begin as bad ownership, bad sizing, or bad route-family selection.

### AI-Collaboration Pressure Test

SignalFlow has become a practical benchmark for AI-assisted architecture. A model can describe a render plausibly and still fail to reason from the underlying geometry. This project makes that failure visible by forcing claims to cash out as owned regions, symbolic paths, terminal clearances, and deterministic glyph composition.

## Side Quest Of The Side Quest

SignalFlow also gives a clean scaffold for testing LLM reasoning. Contemporary language models are often described as machines that can infer structure from text. This project asks a sharper version of that claim: if source code has a geometric signal-flow shape, can an LLM infer that shape from the text alone?

The benchmark is unusually direct because SignalFlow has a deterministic reference renderer. A model can be asked to read source and produce YAML, read YAML and predict geometry zones, read geometry and predict wiring, or skip directly from source to a rendered circuit. Each stage has a concrete artifact to compare against: source -> YAML -> circuit graph -> geometry -> symbolic routes -> realized wiring -> final glyph grid.

That makes partial failure measurable. A model might understand the call graph but lose return identity. It might place chips correctly but put fan-in/out regions too close to terminal labels. It might predict the symbolic route family but fail the glyph algebra at a module boundary. It might describe the render convincingly while drawing orphaned terminals or impossible crossings. Those are not subjective failures of taste; they are mismatches against a deterministic geometric world.

This is the side quest of the side quest: SignalFlow began as a way to make code flow visible, then became a geometry engine, and now also functions as a testbed for whether LLMs can reason through text into spatial structure without hand-waving over the intermediate invariants.

## Input Model

The canonical input format is YAML. Current documents use a top-level `tree` object containing nested call nodes with `module`, `func`, signal labels, optional return labels, and optional `calls`.

Forward-only data-flow examples should omit return labels rather than using empty strings. DAG-style examples may use repeated chip identities to route additional edges into an existing chip. Examples that need visible layer compartments should encode those layers as module names today; synthetic call-stack-depth layer envelopes are a separate rendering mode controlled by `--depthbox`.

## Limitations And Future Work

SignalFlow is an active geometry engine, not a finished compiler pipeline. The current board renderer can materialize substantial world-scale examples, but several areas remain explicit design and implementation targets.

### Internal Within-Chip Wiring

Chip-local `internal_wiring` is supported as a declarative contract, and the older legacy engine contains a richer manifold vocabulary for dense within-chip routing. The board engine still needs a fully unified internal-routing substrate that treats chip interiors with the same symbolic rigor as external world routes. The target is for internal chip wiring, terminal landing geometry, and external board routes to share one coherent ownership model rather than being adjacent systems with partially overlapping rules.

### Cross-World-Zone Wiring

Current world rendering is strongest along harmonized overlap chains where adjacent zones can be solved and inspected with explicit offsets. More general cross-world-zone wiring remains future work: routes that need to traverse non-adjacent zones, bend through larger world grids, or negotiate multiple module/depth envelopes should be represented as first-class world-scale symbolic paths rather than stitched local decisions. The goal is to make multi-zone routing as inspectable as single-zone routing: route family, owned regions, lane identity, crossings, and terminal landings should all survive across the full world.

### Source Compilation

The YAML format is currently the authoritative circuit contract. Compiler output from source languages should eventually produce that contract directly, including stable chip identity, unique port labeling, call order, recursion/back-edge references, and explicit return semantics. The sibling [sftc](https://github.com/rudolphpienaar/sftc) project is the materialized TypeScript-to-SignalFlow-YAML compiler path for that work. Until that compiler path is complete across the full language surface, hand-authored YAML examples remain the cleanest way to exercise the geometry engine.

## Install

```bash
pip install -e .
```

SignalFlow requires Python 3.11 or newer.

## Usage

```bash
# Render a YAML document with the current engine
signalflow examples/show-cohort.yaml

# Read YAML from stdin
cat my_tree.yaml | signalflow -

# Render the built-in example
signalflow --example

# Select an engine explicitly
signalflow --engine new examples/root-multi-child.yaml
signalflow --engine legacy examples/hub.yaml

# Inspect harmonized world geometry or wiring
signalflow examples/simple-circuit/back-and-forth.yaml --geometry
signalflow examples/simple-circuit/back-and-forth.yaml --wiring
signalflow examples/simple-circuit/back-and-forth.yaml --zone 1,2 --wiring
signalflow examples/simple-circuit/back-and-forth.yaml --zones "1,2;1,3" --geometry

# Control structural boundary rendering
signalflow examples/neural-network-4-3-2.yaml --depthbox
signalflow examples/neural-network-4-3-2.yaml --modulepolicy cross
signalflow examples/neural-network-4-3-2.yaml --modulepolicy column
signalflow examples/neural-network-4-3-2.yaml --modulepolicy none

# Compare routing policies
signalflow examples/simple-circuit/back-and-forth.yaml --routing manhattan
signalflow examples/simple-circuit/back-and-forth.yaml --routing paris
signalflow examples/simple-circuit/back-and-forth.yaml --routing none

# Debug the new-engine pipeline
signalflow --engine new --repl examples/simple-circuit/three-deep-linear.yaml
signalflow --engine new --run-snippet snippets/algebraic/hub_kernel_solver.py examples/hub.yaml
signalflow --engine new --repl --load-snippet snippets/algebraic/hub_kernel_solver.py examples/hub.yaml
```

`--engine new` is the default. Zone filters are inspection views, not partial layouts: the full world chain is harmonized first, then the requested zone or zones are selected for display.

